--- Authoritative Engine Documentation ---

## Workflow Engine Configuration Guide

This engine used in AI-powered chat applications to make chats more interactive and intelligent. This is one and only use case for this engine. We can execute some steps of workflow after message, on UI render, on some event, etc.

This document defines the configuration surface for the workflow engine and provides AI-optimized guidance and examples for generating valid workflows.

Each workflow has a entryPoints that will be first node of each execution pathway. You need to think about workflows - starting from entryPoints.

Each workflow has a storage. You can read or modify permanent storage linked to specific chat-workflow instance. 

Nodes executing one after another, step by step. Config is source of truth for pathways. Because we have multiple entryPoints - we can have multiple pathways.

Platform where this engine is used - is a AI-powered chat application with 100k+ characters with personalities, traits, etc. User can attach workflow to dedicated chat with dedicated character. Workflows are designed to be used as AI agents with some pre-built logic to interact with chat context, ui, chat messages. 

### Global Context of Workflows
Any node from workflow can access some details about chat, user, character, etc.
Context is always available in all nodes.

```ts
interface WorkflowContext {
  // Built-in data for chat-related workflows
  chat?: ChatWorkflowData;

  // User-defined data. Can be read anywhere, updated using storage node.
  storage: any | null;

  // Schema for storage data (in json schema format)
  storageSchema?: JSONSchema;

  // Auto-saved node outputs
  $lastOutput?: unknown;
  $outputs?: Record<string, unknown>;
  $prev?: unknown; // Result from the previous node in execution chain

  // Trigger payload for manual/HTTP/UI/chat triggers (standardized)
  triggerPayload?: Record<string, unknown>;

  // Control flags set by nodes to influence outer chat pipeline
  $hardEnd?: boolean; // When true, chat should skip AI call
  $hardEndReason?: string; // Optional reason for hard end

  // Allow additional dynamic properties for user data. You can create some temporary variables here.
  [key: string]: unknown;

  // Storage metadata for workflow execution
  userId?: string; // shortcut for user id for global storage
  chatId?: string; // shortcut for chat id for context
  workflowId?: string; // shortcut for workflow id for storage operations
  isGlobalStorage?: boolean; // whether storage is shared between chats
}

interface ChatWorkflowData {
  char: string; // Full name of character
  user: string; // Full name of user
  chat: {
    id: string;
    userId: string;
    characterId: string;
    title?: string; // Chj
    visibility?: string; // Chat visibility
    isEncrypted?: boolean; // Is chat encrypted
  };
  character: {
    id: string;
    name: string;
    greeting?: string; // Character greeting
    traits?: string; // Character traits
    scenario?: string; // Character scenario
    description?: string; // Character description
    gender?: string; // Character gender
    exampleDialogue?: string; // Character example dialogue
    tags?: Array<{ tag: { name: string; color?: string } }>; // Character tags
  };
  userInfo: {
    id: string;
    name: string;
    email?: string; // User email
    profile?: any; // User profile
  };
  persona?: {
    id: string;
    name: string; // User's persona name
    description?: string; // Persona description
  };
  lastMessage?: {
    id: string;
    content: string; // Last message content
    role: 'user' | 'assistant' | 'system'; // Last message role
    userId: string; // Last message user id
    timestamp: string; // Last message timestamp
  };
  messages?: {
    id: string;
    content: string; // Message content
    role: 'user' | 'assistant' | 'system'; // Message role
    userId: string; // Message user id
    timestamp: string; // Message timestamp
  }[];
}
```

### Storage
Workflow can have storage. Storage is a JSON object that can be read and modified by nodes. Storage is linked to specific chat-workflow instance. But if user wanna change it, storage can be shared between all chats of user. To share set `shareStorageBetweenChats` to `true` in workflow definition.

### Overview and Execution Model
- A workflow is a directed graph of nodes operating over a mutable `WorkflowContext`.
- Each node reads from and writes to the context and returns the next node id (or array for parallel), or `null` to terminate.
- Execution starts at `start` node or from an `entryPoints` mapping. The engine validates the workflow and context up front and after each node.
- Common chaining variables: `$lastOutput`, `$outputs[nodeId]`, `$prev`.

### Expressions and Path Referencing
- Template expressions: `{{ expr }}` - unified syntax for all template expressions
- Safe evaluation with limits: operations (1000), recursion depth (50), timeout (100ms)
- Operators: `===` and `!==` are automatically normalized to `==` and `!=` (loose equality) for compatibility
- Forbidden properties: `__proto__, constructor, prototype, valueOf, toString, hasOwnProperty, isPrototypeOf, propertyIsEnumerable, toLocaleString`
- Paths accepted:
  - Direct access: `{{ fieldName }}` (automatically checks storage)
  - Explicit paths: `{{ storage.fieldName }}` or `{{ chat.lastMessage.content }}`
  - JSON context: `{{ storage.fieldName }}` (full context access)
  - Special: `{{ $lastOutput }}`, `{{ $prev }}`, `{{ $outputs.nodeId }}`
  - **Node references: `{{ @nodeId }}` or `{{ @nodeId.property }}`** - execute standalone nodes on-demand

### Validation, Limits, and Timeouts
- Definition validation: non-empty nodes, one `start` or `entryPoints`, unique IDs, valid `next` refs, valid optional schemas.
- Context validation: if `contextSchema` is provided, AJV (Draft 2020-12) validates initial and post-node context.
- Step limit: `settings.maxSteps` or default `nodes.length × 20`. Exceeding throws `StepLimitExceededError`.
- Timeout: if `settings.timeout` set, the engine computes a deadline and throws on exceed.

### UI Components

UI components are now documented automatically via the workflow generator. The generator includes complete schemas, examples, and type information for all available components.

Key execution points:
- The `ui` node uses `params.templates` to generate components
- Use `{{...}}` expressions in config for dynamic values (access to `item`, `index`, `context`)
- Set appropriate `placement` and `ttl` for each component type
- Components are validated against their schemas at runtime

#### Modal Components

Modal components use `placement: 'modal'` and support rich content arrays with mixed content types:

```json
{
  "type": "modal",
  "placement": "modal",
  "config": {
    "title": "Level Up!",
    "content": [
      { "type": "text", "content": "Congratulations! You reached level 42." },
      { "type": "image", "src": "https://example.com/celebration.jpg", "alt": "Level up", "width": 300 },
      { 
        "type": "progress", 
        "config": { 
          "label": "XP Progress", 
          "value": 850, 
          "max": 1000, 
          "color": "#3b82f6" 
        } 
      },
      { "type": "markdown", "content": "**New abilities unlocked:**\n- Fireball\n- Lightning" }
    ],
    "actions": [
      { "label": "Continue", "variant": "primary", "action": { "type": "send_message", "payload": "continue" } }
    ]
  }
}
```

**Modal content types:**
- `{ type: 'text', content: string }` - Plain text
- `{ type: 'markdown', content: string }` - Markdown formatted text  
- `{ type: 'html', content: string }` - HTML content
- `{ type: 'image', src: string, alt?: string, width?: number, height?: number }` - Images
- `{ type: 'progress', config: ProgressConfig }` - Any UI component with type + config structure

#### Manual Workflow Triggering with HTTP_TRIGGER

The `HTTP_TRIGGER` entrypoint allows workflows to be triggered manually by user actions (e.g., button clicks). This is perfect for implementing manual actions like "Reset Game", "Level Up", "Generate Content", etc.

**Trigger Payload Sources:**
- **POST request:** Payload from user's HTTP request → `context.triggerPayload`
- **UI buttons:** `metadata` object from button action → `context.triggerPayload`
- **Chat triggers:**
  - BEFORE_USER_MESSAGE: `{ text: string, messageId: string }`
  - BEFORE_CHAT_MESSAGE: `{ messageId: string }`

**UI Button Metadata:**
UI buttons can pass structured data via `metadata` field:
- `action` - Action identifier for routing
- `workflowId` - Optional: trigger different workflow
- `entrypoint` - Optional: specify entrypoint in target workflow
- Any custom fields - Available in `triggerPayload`

**Configuration Pattern EXAMPLE:**
actionRouter is highly recommended to use for routing actions based on triggerPayload. It is a good practice to use it with `if` node.

```json
{
  "entryPoints": {
    "HTTP_TRIGGER": "actionRouter"
  },
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "actionRouter",
      "type": "if",
      "params": {
        "condition": "triggerPayload.action === 'reset_game'",
        "ifTrue": "resetGameNode",
        "ifFalse": "otherActions"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

**UI Button Integration:**
```json
{
  "type": "button",
  "config": {
    "label": "Reset Game",
    "variant": "destructive",
    "action": {
      "type": "trigger_workflow",
      "payload": "reset",
      "metadata": {
        "action": "reset_game",
        "workflowId": "game-actions-v2",
        "entrypoint": "RESET",
        "confirm": true
      }
    }
  }
}
```

**HTTP_TRIGGER → Sub-Workflow Pattern:**
Use HTTP_TRIGGER to route actions to different sub-workflows:

```jsonc
{
  "entryPoints": {
    "HTTP_TRIGGER": "actionRouter"
  },
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "actionRouter",
      "type": "switch",
      "params": {
        "value": "{{ triggerPayload.action }}",
        "cases": [
          { "case": "reset_game", "next": "callResetWorkflow" },
          { "case": "level_up", "next": "callLevelUpWorkflow" },
          { "case": "save_progress", "next": "callSaveWorkflow" }
        ],
        "default": "unknownAction"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "callResetWorkflow",
      "type": "workflow",
      "params": {
        "workflowId": "game-reset-logic",
        "payload": {
          "confirm": "{{ triggerPayload.confirm }}",
          "resetType": "{{ triggerPayload.resetType || 'full' }}"
        }
      },
      "next": "sendConfirmation"
    }
  ]
}
```

**Common Use Cases:**
- Game actions (reset, level up, save progress)
- Content generation (create story, generate image)
- Data management (clear cache, export data)
- User preferences (toggle settings, change theme)
- Modal dialogs for confirmations, notifications, and rewards (use placement: 'modal')
- Route to different sub-workflows based on action type

### Scheduled Execution with CRON Entrypoint

The `CRON` entrypoint fires on cron expressions defined in `settings.cronSchedules`. Map it to the first node to run on each tick and list the schedules under workflow settings.

- `cron` — standard expression (no seconds field)
- `entryPoint` — optional override if you want to reuse the same schedule for another entrypoint (defaults to `CRON`)
- `timezone` — per-schedule override (falls back to workflow/global timezone)
- `payload` — JSON payload merged into `context.triggerPayload`

```jsonc
{
  "entryPoints": {
    "CRON": "nightlySweep"
  },
  "settings": {
    "cronSchedules": [
      {
        "id": "nightly-maintenance",
        "cron": "0 2 * * *",
        "timezone": "America/Los_Angeles",
        "payload": { "run": "nightly" }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

At runtime the cron scheduler service locates all chats that attach this workflow, checks activity within the last three days, and runs the mapped entrypoint with the configured payload.

### Authoring Checklist
- Provide exactly one `start` node or configure `entryPoints`.
- Ensure all `next` references exist; IDs are unique.
- Decide on step limit: set `settings.maxSteps` or rely on default `nodes × 20`.
- If using `storage.mode = 'ai'`, provide a clear `aiPrompt` and optional `storageSchema` in context.
- Use `map` for array formatting; use `set` node to write computed values to context.
- For `ui`, prefer `templates` and `{{}}` config placeholders; set `returnValue` if the caller expects `$lastOutput`.

## Data Node and Standalone Node References

### Data Node
The `data` node returns static or computed data. Supports both `value` (static/mixed) and `expression` (computed) parameters:

```json
{
  "id": "config",
  "type": "data",
  "params": {
    "value": {
      "apiUrl": "https://api.example.com",
      "userName": "{{ chat.user }}",
      "level": "{{ storage.level || 1 }}"
    }
  }
}
```

### Standalone Node References: `{{ @nodeId }}`
Reference any node by ID using `@` prefix. The node executes on-demand when referenced:

- `{{ @nodeId }}` - Execute node and return its full output (string, object, array, etc.)
- `{{ @nodeId.property }}` - Access specific property of node output object
- `{{ @nodeId.nested.property }}` - Access deeply nested properties

**Examples:**
- `{{ @userInfo }}` returns entire result: `"Hello John!"` or `{"name": "John", "level": 5}`
- `{{ @userInfo.name }}` returns just the name: `"John"`
- `{{ @config.database.host }}` returns nested property: `"localhost"`

### Example: Standalone Configuration
```json
{
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "mainFlow",
      "type": "sendMessage", 
      "params": {
        "message": "User: {{ @greeting }} | Name: {{ @userInfo.name }} | API: {{ @config.apiUrl }}"
      }
    },
    // Standalone nodes (no next, no entryPoints)
    {
      "id": "greeting",
      "type": "data",
      "params": {
        "expression": "Hello {{ chat.user }}!"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "userInfo",
      "type": "data",
      "params": {
        "value": {
          "name": "{{ chat.user }}",
          "level": "{{ storage.level || 1 }}"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "config", 
      "type": "data",
      "params": {
        "value": {
          "apiUrl": "https://api.example.com",
          "timeout": 5000
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "entryPoints": { "HTTP_TRIGGER": "mainFlow" }
}
```

**Result:** `"User: Hello John! | Name: John | API: https://api.example.com"`
## Variable Management with Set Node (formerly Transform)

### Set Node - Setting Values in Context

The `set` node is the primary way to set values in the workflow context. It evaluates expressions and assigns values to specified paths.

**Note:** The `transform` node type is deprecated but still supported for backward compatibility. Use `set` for all new workflows.

#### Basic Usage

```json
{
  "id": "setValue",
  "type": "set",
  "params": {
    "path": "/myVariable",
    "value": "Hello World"
  }
}
```

#### With Expression Evaluation

```json
{
  "id": "calculateValue",
  "type": "set",
  "params": {
    "path": "/calculated",
    "value": "{{ storage.score * 2 + 10 }}"
  }
}
```

#### Dynamic Path

```json
{
  "id": "dynamicPath",
  "type": "set",
  "params": {
    "path": "{{ '/user/' + chat.user + '/status' }}",
    "value": "active"
  }
}
```

#### Complex Object Assignment

```json
{
  "id": "setUserData",
  "type": "set",
  "params": {
    "path": "/userData",
    "value": {
      "name": "{{ chat.user }}",
      "level": "{{ storage.level || 1 }}",
      "timestamp": "{{ $now() }}",
      "messages": "{{ length(chat.messages) }}"
    }
  }
}
```

#### Setting $lastOutput

```json
{
  "id": "setOutput",
  "type": "set",
  "params": {
    "path": "/$lastOutput",
    "value": "This will be available as $lastOutput in next nodes"
  }
}
```

## Array Operations in Storage

### Sort Arrays In-Place
Use `sort` operation to sort arrays directly in storage:

```json
{
  "type": "storage",
  "params": {
    "operations": [
      { "op": "sort", "path": "guilds", "field": "rating", "order": "desc" }
    ]
  }
}
```

### Update Array Items by Filter
Use `update_by_filter` to update items matching criteria:

```json
{
  "type": "storage",
  "params": {
    "operations": [
      { "op": "update_by_filter", "path": "guilds", "filter": { "id": "playerGuild" }, "value": { "rating": 900 } }
    ]
  }
}
```

### Bracket Notation for Array Access
Access array elements by index using bracket notation:

```json
{ "op": "set", "path": "guilds[0].rating", "value": 1000 }
```

### Remove Array Items
Remove items from arrays using index or filter. Both support template expressions.

**Remove by Index:**
```json
{
  "type": "storage",
  "params": {
    "operations": [
      { "op": "remove", "path": "deck", "value": "{{ cardIndex }}" }
    ]
  }
}
```

**Remove by Filter (with template expressions):**
```json
{
  "type": "storage",
  "params": {
    "operations": [
      { "op": "remove", "path": "deck", "filter": { "name": "{{ drawnCard.name }}" } }
    ]
  }
}
```

**Remove using sourcePath:**
```json
{
  "type": "storage",
  "params": {
    "operations": [
      { "op": "remove", "path": "deck", "sourcePath": "/selectedIndex" }
    ]
  }
}
```

### Expression Functions for Arrays
- `findBy(array, field, value)` - Find first item where field equals value
- `findIndexBy(array, field, value)` - Find index of first matching item
- `sort(array, field, order)` - Return sorted copy of array
- `filter(array, field, op, value)` - Filter array by condition

**Examples:**
```
{{ findBy(storage.guilds, 'id', 'playerGuild') }}  // Returns guild object or null
{{ findIndexBy(storage.guilds, 'id', 'playerGuild') }}  // Returns index or -1
{{ sort(storage.guilds, 'rating', 'desc') }}  // Returns sorted copy
```

## Sub-Workflow Node

### Overview
The `workflow` node allows you to call another workflow from within a workflow, creating modular and reusable workflow logic. Context, storage, and chat data are shared between parent and sub-workflow.

### Parameters

- **workflowId** (string, required) - ID of the workflow to execute
- **entrypoint** (string, optional) - Entrypoint name OR specific node-id to start from
- **payload** (object, optional) - Custom data passed to sub-workflow's `triggerPayload`
- **returnPath** (string, optional) - Path to extract result from finalContext (default: `$lastOutput`)
- **outputAs** (string, optional) - Variable name to save the result in parent context
- **isolateStorage** (boolean, optional) - If true, sub-workflow storage changes are NOT merged back to parent

### How It Works

1. **Call** - Parent workflow calls sub-workflow with shared or isolated context
2. **Storage** - If `isolateStorage=true`, loads sub-workflow's own storage from `chatWorkflow` table
3. **Payload** - Custom `payload` merged into sub-workflow's `triggerPayload`
4. **Execution** - Sub-workflow executes and can modify storage
5. **Save** - If `isolateStorage=true`, saves sub-workflow storage to its own `chatWorkflow` record
6. **Merge** - Storage changes merged back to parent (unless `isolateStorage=true`)
7. **Return** - Result extracted via `returnPath` and saved to `outputAs`

### Examples

**Simple reusable validation:**
```jsonc
{
  "id": "validateInput",
  "type": "workflow",
  "params": {
    "workflowId": "content-filter-v1"
  },
  "next": "processMessage"
}
```

**Pass data via payload:**
```jsonc
{
  "id": "checkSpam",
  "type": "workflow",
  "params": {
    "workflowId": "spam-detector",
    "payload": {
      "text": "{{ chat.lastMessage.content }}",
      "userId": "{{ chat.userInfo.id }}",
      "rules": ["spam", "toxic", "nsfw"]
    },
    "outputAs": "spamCheckResult"
  },
  "next": "handleResult"
}
```

**Extract structured data with returnPath:**
```jsonc
{
  "id": "calculateDamage",
  "type": "workflow",
  "params": {
    "workflowId": "battle-calculator",
    "entrypoint": "CALCULATE_DAMAGE",
    "payload": {
      "attacker": "{{ storage.player }}",
      "defender": "{{ storage.enemy }}",
      "skill": "{{ storage.selectedSkill }}"
    },
    "returnPath": "storage.battleResult.totalDamage",
    "outputAs": "damageDealt"
  },
  "next": "applyDamage"
}
```

**Conditional workflow execution:**
```jsonc
{
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "checkLevel",
      "type": "if",
      "params": {
        "condition": "storage.level >= 10",
        "ifTrue": "advancedLogic",
        "ifFalse": "basicLogic"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "advancedLogic",
      "type": "workflow",
      "params": {
        "workflowId": "advanced-game-mechanics",
        "payload": { "level": "{{ storage.level }}" }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "basicLogic",
      "type": "workflow",
      "params": {
        "workflowId": "basic-game-mechanics"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

### UI Button → Sub-Workflow Pattern

UI buttons can trigger workflows via HTTP_TRIGGER entrypoint. Metadata from button is available in `triggerPayload`:

**Button configuration:**
```jsonc
{
  "type": "button",
  "config": {
    "label": "Reset Game",
    "variant": "destructive",
    "action": {
      "type": "trigger_workflow",
      "metadata": {
        "action": "reset_game",
        "workflowId": "game-actions-v2",
        "entrypoint": "RESET",
        "confirm": true
      }
    }
  }
}
```

**Workflow HTTP_TRIGGER handler with actionRouter pattern:**
```jsonc
{
  "entryPoints": {
    "HTTP_TRIGGER": "actionRouter"
  },
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "actionRouter",
      "type": "if",
      "params": {
        "condition": "triggerPayload.action === 'reset_game'",
        "ifTrue": "callResetWorkflow",
        "ifFalse": "otherActions"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "callResetWorkflow",
      "type": "workflow",
      "params": {
        "workflowId": "game-reset-logic",
        "payload": {
          "confirm": "{{ triggerPayload.confirm }}",
          "resetType": "full"
        }
      },
      "next": "showConfirmation"
    }
  ]
}
```

### Access Sub-Workflow Data

**In sub-workflow:**
- Access payload via `{{ triggerPayload.fieldName }}`
- All parent context, chat data available
- If `isolateStorage=false` (default): Shares parent's storage, changes merge back
- If `isolateStorage=true` + `shareStorageBetweenChats=false`: Uses own per-chat storage from `chatWorkflow` table
- If `isolateStorage=true` + `shareStorageBetweenChats=true`: Uses global storage from `UserWorkflowStorage` table (shared across ALL user's chats)

**In parent workflow:**
- Access result via `outputAs` variable: `{{ damageDealt }}`
- Or via `$lastOutput` if no `outputAs` specified
- If `isolateStorage=false`: Storage changes visible immediately after sub-workflow
- If `isolateStorage=true`: Parent storage remains unchanged, data passed via `payload`

### Limitations & Protection

- **Max depth:** 10 levels of nested sub-workflows
- **Circular detection:** Prevents workflow A → B → A loops
- **Shared storage:** Changes in sub-workflow affect parent by default (use `isolateStorage=true` to prevent)
- **Isolated storage:** Sub-workflow with `isolateStorage=true` gets its own storage record (auto-created if needed)
  - Per-chat: `chatWorkflow` table if `shareStorageBetweenChats=false`
  - Global: `UserWorkflowStorage` table if `shareStorageBetweenChats=true`
- **Entrypoint validation:** Both named entrypoints and node-ids supported
- **Error handling:** Sub-workflow errors propagate to parent with context

### Common Use Cases

1. **Reusable validation logic** - Content filters, spam detection, rule checks
2. **Modular architecture** - Split large workflows into smaller, focused pieces
3. **Conditional execution** - Different workflows for different user levels/states
4. **UI-triggered actions** - Buttons trigger mini-workflows for specific tasks
5. **Calculation modules** - Damage, score, price calculations as sub-workflows
6. **Per-chat statistics** - Use `isolateStorage=true` to track stats within one chat
7. **Global statistics** - Use `isolateStorage=true` + `shareStorageBetweenChats=true` to track stats across ALL user's chats

## Other useful nodes

**Image Generation:**
```jsonc
{
  "nodes": [
    { "id": "start", "type": "start", "next": "generateImage" },
    {
      "id": "generateImage",
      "type": "imageGeneration",
      "params": {
        "prompt": "{{message.content}}",
        "style": "realistic-vision-v5",
        "action": "sendToChat",
        "messageText": "some random text will be sent to chat with image",
        "outputAs": "generatedImage"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

**Advanced Image Generation with Custom Instructions:**
```jsonc
{
  "nodes": [
    { "id": "start", "type": "start", "next": "enhancePrompt" },
    {
      "id": "enhancePrompt",
      "type": "agent",
      "params": {
        "prompt": "Enhance this image prompt for better results: {{message.content}}",
        "model": "gpt-5-mini",
        "outputs": { "enhancedPrompt": "/enhancedPrompt" }
      },
      "next": "generateImage"
    },
    {
      "id": "generateImage",
      "type": "imageGeneration",
      "params": {
        "prompt": "{{enhancedPrompt}}",
        "customInstructions": "High quality, detailed, cinematic lighting",
        "action": "sendToChat",
        "messageText": "some random text",
        "outputAs": "generatedImage"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

**Character Avatar Update Workflow:**
```jsonc
{
  "nodes": [
    { "id": "start", "type": "start", "next": "generateAvatar" },
    {
      "id": "generateAvatar",
      "type": "imageGeneration",
      "params": {
        "prompt": "Professional avatar of {{character.name}}: {{character.descriptions.EN}}",
        "style": "realistic-vision-v5",
        "action": "updateAvatar",
        "outputAs": "newAvatar"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

### Performance & Best Practices

**Schema Validation (for AI updates):**
- Define defaults for all fields relied upon by prompts
- Constrain with `minimum`/`maximum`/`enum` where possible  
- Use `required` for essentials; `additionalProperties: false` for strict objects
- Attach storage schema at runtime as `context.storageSchema` for validation

**Performance Optimization:**
- Place frequent conditions early (`if`/`switch`) to short-circuit
- Batch related storage updates in a single `storage` node
- Reuse computed results via `output` aliasing and `$outputs[nodeId]`
- Set `settings.timeout` globally and `timeoutMs` per `httpRequest`
- Prefer `switch` over deeply nested `if` chains

**Common Anti-Patterns (avoid):**
- Inventing params not in schemas (e.g., `ui.components`, `transform.template`)
- Omitting required fields like `httpRequest.outputPath`
- Using `map` without `outputAs` (results will be lost)
- Embedding sensitive data in plain context

## Tips

- Provide entryPoints. Do not use start/end unless explicitly required by the user.
- Ensure node ids are unique; next references exist or are null.
- Use node params exactly as documented in the schemas below.
- Do not invent params - follow the node schemas strictly.
- Include settings.timeout and settings.maxSteps.
- Return compact, valid JSON that passes the provided schema.
- If ANY node has type "ui", you MUST set entryPoints.UI_REQUEST to that ui node id.
- If ANY node needs to share context with chat, you MUST set entryPoints.CONTEXT_REQUEST to that node id.
- If workflow should be triggered manually (e.g., from button), set entryPoints.HTTP_TRIGGER to the start node id.
- HTTP_TRIGGER and other triggers can access `triggerPayload` in context for custom data from the trigger request or chat pipeline.

## Hard End Node (Stopping AI)

Use the `hard_end` node to immediately terminate a workflow and tell the chat pipeline to skip sending the user message to the LLM (no AI answer). This is useful if your workflow handles the message itself (e.g., runs commands, sends a response via `sendMessage` node, updates storage/UI) and you do not want a character reply.

Behavior:
- Sets `context.$hardEnd = true` and, if provided, `context.$hardEndReason`.
- The chat pipeline will respect this flag before the AI call and will skip the LLM request.
- You can combine with `BEFORE_USER_MESSAGE` to preprocess the raw user text, and/or with `BEFORE_CHAT_MESSAGE` to short-circuit just before AI.

Good example snippet:
```jsonc
{
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "checkCommand",
      "type": "if",
      "params": {
        "lhsPath": "/triggerPayload/text",
        "op": "startsWith",
        "rhs": "/roll",
        "then": "handle",
        "else": "continue"
      }
    },
    { "id": "handle", "type": "sendMessage", "params": { "text": "🎲 You rolled a 6!" }, "next": "stop" },
    { "id": "stop", "type": "hard_end", "params": { "reason": "Command handled by workflow" } }
  ],
  "entryPoints": { "BEFORE_USER_MESSAGE": "checkCommand" }
}
```

Data access cheat sheet:
- Last message: {{ chat.lastMessage.content }}
- Recent history: {{ formatHistory(chat.messages, 3) }}
- User / Character names: {{ chat.user }}, {{ chat.char }}
- Storage fields: {{ storage.fieldName }}
- Prior node outputs: {{ $outputs.nodeId }} and {{ $lastOutput }}
- In chat messages you cant inject images using markdown, use imageGeneration node instead
- UI template config uses {{...}} with item/index available (e.g., {{item}}, {{index}})
- Data from any trigger are available in {{triggerPayload.fieldName}}. For example, key `text` is user message text sent to chat in case if trigger is BEFORE_USER_MESSAGE.

## Simple workflow example - HP Tracker
This workflow is a simple example of how to use storage to track user's HP across single chat.

Has three entryPoints:
1. UI_REQUEST - to display HP bar on UI
2. CHAT_MESSAGE - to update HP based on user's message
3. CONTEXT_REQUEST - to format context for chat. AI character will see this context in chat. It will be added to context of chat. We will always use $lastOutput to get final context text.

Has three nodes:
1. formatForContext - to format context for chat using set node. AI character will see this context in chat. It will be added to context of chat.
2. updateHP - to update HP based on user's message, storage node combine storage operations in AI mode.
3. displayHPBar - to display HP bar on UI

**Workflow definition:**
```jsonc
{
  "id": "hpTrackerWorkflow",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "formatForContext",
      "next": null,
      "type": "set",
      "params": {
        "path": "/contextText",
        "value": "**User HP Status:**\n❤️ HP: {{ storage.hpData.currentHP || 100 }}/100\n📊 Status: {{ storage.hpData.status || 'normal' }}\n📉 Last Change: {{ storage.hpData.lastLoss || 0 }} HP ({{ storage.hpData.lastReason || 'initial state' }})\n\n**Recent HP History:**\n{{ storage.hpData.history ? formatArray(storage.hpData.history, '• ${hpChange} HP: ${reason} (User: ${chat.user})', '\n', 5, true) : 'No history yet' }}\n\nThe user's health changes based on their activity patterns and engagement."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "updateHP",
      "next": "displayHPBar",
      "type": "storage",
      "params": {
        "mode": "ai",
        "model": "gpt-5-mini",
        "aiPrompt": "Calculate HP loss and update storage based on user activity:\n\n**Recent Message:**\n{{ formatHistory($json.chat.messages, 2) }}\n\n**User:** {{ chat.user }}\n\nCalculate HP loss:\n- Long messages (>100 chars): -10 HP, reason: \"Long message activity\"\n- Medium messages (50-100 chars): -5 HP, reason: \"Medium message activity\"\n- Short messages (<50 chars): -3 HP, reason: \"Short message activity\"\n- Rapid/spam messages: -15 HP, reason: \"Rapid messaging detected\"\n- Commands or special actions: -20 HP, reason: \"Special command usage\"\n- Recovery actions (+5 HP): reason: \"Positive interaction\"\n\nReturn JSON operations to update storage:\n[\n  {\"op\": \"set\", \"path\": \"hpData.currentHP\", \"value\": [new HP value, minimum 0]},\n  {\"op\": \"set\", \"path\": \"hpData.lastLoss\", \"value\": [HP lost/gained]},\n  {\"op\": \"set\", \"path\": \"hpData.lastReason\", \"value\": \"[reason for change]\"},\n  {\"op\": \"set\", \"path\": \"hpData.status\", \"value\": \"critical/low/normal/high\"},\n  {\"op\": \"add_to_array\", \"path\": \"hpData.history\", \"value\": {\"hpChange\": [HP change amount], \"reason\": \"[reason]\", \"newHP\": [new HP value]}}\n]\n\nEnsure HP never goes below 0. Add history entry for every change.",
        "forceChanges": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "displayHPBar",
      "next": null,
      "type": "ui",
      "params": {
        "outputAs": "uiComponents",
        "templates": [
          {
            "type": "progress",
            "config": {
              "max": 100,
              "min": 0,
              "color": "{{ storage.hpData.currentHP > 50 ? \"#10b981\" : storage.hpData.currentHP > 20 ? \"#f59e0b\" : \"#ef4444\" }}",
              "label": "❤️ HP",
              "style": {
                "height": "lg",
                "variant": "striped"
              },
              "value": "{{ storage.hpData.currentHP || 100 }}",
              "format": "custom",
              "animated": true,
              "showValue": true,
              "customFormat": "{{ storage.hpData.currentHP || 100 }}/100 HP"
            },
            "metadata": {
              "ttl": 3600
            },
            "placement": "above_input"
          }
        ],
        "returnValue": "uiComponents"
      }
    }
  ],
  "settings": {
    "name": "HP Tracker System",
    "shareStorageBetweenChats": false
  },
  "entryPoints": {
    "UI_REQUEST": "displayHPBar",
    "CHAT_MESSAGE": "updateHP",
    "CONTEXT_REQUEST": "formatForContext"
  }
}
```

**Storage schema:**
```jsonc
{
  "hpData": {
    "type": "object",
    "required": ["currentHP", "status"],
    "properties": {
      "currentHP": {
        "type": "number",
        "default": 100,
        "minimum": 0,
        "maximum": 100,
        "description": "Current HP of user"
      },
      "status": {
        "type": "string",
        "enum": ["critical", "low", "normal", "high"],
        "default": "high",
        "description": "HP status level"
      },
      "lastLoss": {
        "type": "number",
        "default": 0,
        "description": "Last amount of HP lost or gained (negative for loss, positive 
  for gain)"
      },
      "lastReason": {
        "type": "string",
        "default": "initial state",
        "description": "Reason for last HP change"
      },
      "history": {
        "type": "array",
        "default": [],
        "description": "History of HP changes",
        "items": {
          "type": "object",
          "required": ["hpChange", "reason", "newHP"],
          "properties": {
            "hpChange": {
              "type": "number",
              "description": "Amount of HP changed (negative for loss, positive for 
  gain)"
            },
            "reason": {
              "type": "string",
              "description": "Reason for the change"
            },
            "newHP": {
              "type": "number",
              "minimum": 0,
              "maximum": 100,
              "description": "HP value after the change"
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "additionalProperties": false
  }
}
```


